data: {"choices":[{"delta":{"reasoning_content":"\u003cAppShell\u003e            (flex column, 100vh)\n  \u003cHeader title=\"Colour Greeting\" /\u003e\n  \u003cmain or div\u003e       (content area — needs flex: 1 and centering)\n    ...greeting + buttons...\n  \u003c/main\u003e\n  \u003cFooter\u003e...\u003c/Footer\u003e\n\u003c/AppShell\u003e\n```\n\nThe footer has margin-top: auto which pushes it to the bottom. For the content area, I'll use a `\u003cmain\u003e` with flex: 1, display: flex, flex-direction: column, align-items: center, justify-content: center. Then the h1 and a row of buttons with a gap.\n\nNow the greeting color: `color: var(--color-cat-1)` etc. via inline style — inline style with `color: \"var(--color-cat-1)\"` uses the token, no hex. That's the cleanest: keep selected token name in state.\n\nState: `useState(\"--color-cat-1\")` storing the token name; buttons set it. Colors mapping:\n- Blue → --color-cat-1 (default)\n- Orange → --color-cat-6\n- Violet → --color-cat-5\n\nFiles to write:\n\n1. `src/main.jsx` — entry, imports styles once:\n```jsx\nimport { StrictMode } from \"react\";\nimport { createRoot } from \"react-dom/client\";\nimport \"@codesweep-ai/ui/styles/core.css\";\nimport \"@codesweep-ai/ui/styles/components/app-shell.css\";\nimport \"@codesweep-ai/ui/styles/components/button.css\";\nimport \"./index.css\";\nimport App from \"./App.jsx\";\n\ncreateRoot(document.getElementById(\"root\")).render(\n  \u003cStrictMode\u003e\n    \u003cApp /\u003e\n  \u003c/StrictMode\u003e\n);\n```\n\nWait — brief says \"Import the styles once at the entry point\" listing the three sheets. My own index.css for layout is separate and fine (the app needs centering CSS; no hex values though — I only use tokens and layout properties).\n\n2. `src/App.jsx`:\n```jsx\nimport { useState } from \"react\";\nimport { AppShell, Header, Footer, Button } from \"@codesweep-ai/ui\";\n\nconst COLOURS = [\n  { label: \"Blue\", token: \"--color-cat-1\" },\n  { label: \"Orange\", token: \"--color-cat-6\" },\n  { label: \"Violet\", token: \"--color-cat-5\" },\n];\n\nexport default function App() {\n  const [colour, setColour] = useState(COLOURS[0].token);\n\n  return (\n    \u003cAppShell\u003e\n      \u003cHeader title=\"Colour Greeting\" /\u003e\n      \u003cmain className=\"content\"\u003e\n        \u003ch1 className=\"greeting\" style={{ color: `var(${colour})` }}\u003e\n          Hello, world!\n        \u003c/h1\u003e\n        \u003cdiv className=\"button-row\"\u003e\n          {COLOURS.map(({ label, token }) =\u003e (\n            \u003cButton\n              key={token}\n              variant=\"secondary\"\n              size=\"md\"\n              onClick={() =\u003e setColour(token)}\n            \u003e\n              {label}\n            \u003c/Button\u003e\n          ))}\n        \u003c/div\u003e\n      \u003c/main\u003e\n      \u003cFooter\u003eBuilt with the codesweep design system\u003c/Footer\u003e\n    \u003c/AppShell\u003e\n  );\n}\n```\n\n3. `src/index.css`:\n```css\n.content {\n  flex: 1;\n  display: flex;\n  flex-direction: column;\n  align-items: center;\n  justify-content: center;\n  gap: var(--space-6, 24px);\n}\n\n.greeting {\n  margin: 0;\n  font-size: ...;\n}\n\n.button-row {\n  display: flex;\n  gap: var(--space-3);\n  justify-content: center;\n}\n```\n\nHmm, should I use DS tokens like var(--space-*) in my CSS? The tokens exist (core.css holds tokens). Using var(--space-3) etc. is safe with fallbacks. Keep it simple: use token references with fallbacks for gaps. Actually \"small even gap between them\" — I'll use `var(--space-3, 0.75rem)`. For the h1 \"single large heading\" — set a large font-size, maybe `var(--text-4xl, 2.25rem)`... I don't know the exact token names. Let me check core.css/tokens.css for font-size tokens quickly. Simpler: just use a plain `font-size: 3rem;`? That's not a color so hex restriction doesn't apply, but let me check if there are font-size tokens to be idiomatic. Let me grep tokens.css for font-size.\n\nAlso check core.css includes tokens (does core.css import tokens.css?). The brief says \"The core sheet holds the tokens\" — so core.css includes the token definitions. Good.\n\nLet me check font-size token names."},"finish_reason":null,"index":0,"raw_output":null}],"created":1793343009,"id":"chatcmpl-286e31ce","model":"accounts/fireworks/models/kimi-k3","object":"chat.completion.chunk","usage":null}

